{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789550233,"nonce":"QQFHHP7q/H1P70OaELJ0BkxDTjc=","message":"Contrast with ZynML nbody: benchmarks/bench_nbody.zynml uses Array\u003cBody\u003e, where Body has seven f64 fields. Its optimized advance HIR carries Body{f64...} values, fsub/fmul/fdiv and intrinsic.sqrt in the pair loop. Python speed/kernels/nbody_modified.py instead builds BODIES as a dict of (position list, velocity list, mass) tuples and PAIRS as precomputed aliases to those tuples. Python type inference sees bodies and pairs as List(Object); the Python advance HIR retains generic arithmetic/indexed access calls. This is a frontend representation/type-specialization gap, not evidence that the shared native float backend regressed. Python benchmarks/bench_nbody.py is closer structurally to ZynML (Body class) but its current inferred fields x/y/z/vx/vy/vz are still Object despite annotations, so a source port alone does not recover ZynML's f64 path. ZynML computes 10,000,000 advances per run; nbody_modified computes 20,000 steps per timed iteration, so absolute times are not directly comparable. ZynML uses intrinsic.sqrt; modified Python uses ** -1.5. In a temporary 10-trial zypy diagnostic, replacing the exponent with an equivalent sqrt formula measured 365.39 ms original vs 370.18 ms sqrt, so that substitution alone did not help. Explicit float casts on eight unpacked scalars measured 464.62 ms; conversions alone are not a fix. These temporary variants were timing probes, not checked benchmark changes. ZynML HIR: /tmp/zyntax_zynml_nbody_hir/000-post-opt-compile_module.hir; Python HIR: /tmp/zyntax_nbody_hir/001-post-opt-tiered-compile_module.hir.","files":null}]}